          HOW TO PLAY THE GAMES OF KALAH           Program by Michael J. KnudsenThe KALAH program is based on an ancient BedouinArab game of the same name, plus an American gamemarketed under the name of "Dead-Pan" in the 1950s.Our Kalah is two games in one; the method of playis the same but the object of the two games isdifferent. Both are contests of skill and strategy,with no random luck involved.The Kalah program pits you against the computer. Atstartup, you have three options: which game toplay, what skill level the computer will play at,and who goes first. Each time it is your turn, youmay make a move, ask the computer for advice,re-display the board, or quit this game and startanother of either type.THE PLAYING BOARD and RULESEach player has six "bins" in a row on his/her/itsside, numbered 1 thru 6, plus an extra "pit" to theright of the bins. The computer's pit is on yourleft, but for your convenience the computer reportsits moves using the same left-to-right bin numbersas you do.A game begins with 3 men or "stones" in each of the12 bins. You both alternate making moves asfollows:(1) Pick up ALL the stones in one of YOUR bins. Youmay not move from an empty (zero) bin nor from aPit.(2) Moving to your right, counter-clockwise, dropexactly one stone in each bin you pass. Think ofthe bins and two pits as in a big circle. If you gooff your right side, drop one in your Pit; thenstart dropping into the computer's bins, nowtraveling right to left. If you reach the left edgeof those bins, drop one in the computer's Pit andcontinue on to your bin #1, etc. If this isunclear, play the program for a few moves to getthe picture.(3) Your move ends when the last stone is dropped.It MUST land in either a Pit or a NON-EMPTY bin. Alot of Kalah strategy involves "defensive" movesthat clear out a bin just to keep your opponentfrom making a move that would land there. Thecomputer will tell you when your move is illegal,and wait for you to type another.(4) There are no bonus extra turns -- just one moveper turn.(5) If a player has no legal move, he is "blocked"and loses that turn. Otherwise each player MUSTmake a move; "passing" turns is not allowed.(6) If neither player can move, the game is"deadlocked" and is won or tied as described below.OBJECT OF THE GAME(S)When you execute KALAH, the first prompt askswhether to play Pigeons or Kalah. "Pigeons" is theDead-Pan-like version. You win at Pigeons byemptying out all your bins first, or, in case ofdeadlock, by having fewer total stones left on yourside, NOT counting the Pits. The computer displaysthe Pigeons board without even showing how manystones have fallen into the Pits, whose onlyfunction is to eat up stones and bring the gamegradually to an end. Neither Pit "belongs" toanyone, so they are shown as empty () in the centerrow that carries the bin number labels. To theright of each row of bins, the display shows thetotal number of stones in each player's bins.If you choose Kalah, the bin totals are not shown.Instead, numbers appear within the Pits' () whichtell how many stones have collected in them. Theobject of Kalah is not to unload your bins, but tofill your right-hand Pit with more stones than youropponent's, and to have more there when neitherplayer can move anymore, which ends the game. Notethat your Pit is displayed in line with your bins,and likewise for the computer's, to remind you oftheir ownership and importance.RUNNING THE PROGRAMSimply execute the binary program "kalah." Thefirst option is to choose between Pigeons or Kalah,or to return to OS-9 without hitting BREAK. Nextyou will be prompted for the computer's skilllevel, which is the number of moves it looks aheadin considering its next move.  Levels range from 2,very fast and easy to beat, to 12, which isprobably unbeatable and takes several minutes toponder each move. If you type a number outside thisrange, it will be taken as 2 or 12. Finally, theprogram displays the initial board and gives youthe choice of making the first move or followingthe machine.Now the game is under way. Whenever it is yourturn, you have four options:     Type 1 thru 6 to make a move from that bin;     Type 0 to re-display the present board;     Type -1 to abandon ths game and start theprogram over;     Or type -2 thru -12 to ask the computer whatmove it would make in your position if playing atthe skill level following the - sign.  You mayrepeat this at various levels as long as you like,but the board doesn't change till you give a legalmove.Other numeric inputs will get an error message,as will moves that are illegal at the moment.Then you get another chance, and so on.After your move, the updated playing board isprinted and the program computes its move. Whilethe program is thinking, the screen cursor hopsback and forth at odd intervals to assure you thatthe game is alive and well. (This works on the CoCoand any terminal that uses Ctrl-F for cursor-rightand Ctrl-H for backspace). Then the computer printsits move and the updated board and prompts for yournext move, and so on.The program detects when either it or you areblocked (no legal moves).  If you are blocked, theprogram waits till you hit ENTER before  its nextmove, so you can watch what's happening (no, itnever cheats; neither can you!).  When neither ofyou can move, the stones are counted to find thewinner.  In Pigeons, clearing all of one's bins isan instant win.RANDOM NOTES & HINTSTo get started, choose Pigeons at skill level 4 andlet the machine go first.  On your turns, alwaysmake the leftmost legal move -- just hit keys from1 to 6 until you hit a legal bin.  Stop andunderstand the board changes, and why certain movesare illegal.  This simple strategy will win Pigeonsat level 4.Pigeons is an intuitively easier game to play thanKalah, because moving a binfull of stones from yourboard to the other side both helps clear your boardand at the same time makes more work for youropponent.  In Kalah, you want to keep stones onyour side to end up in your Pit; anything that goesaround your Pit can be used by your opponent.Whenever the computer reports its move, it alsoincludes a "worth $N" that tells how many stonesahead or behind you it expects to be at thatpoint.  You also get this estimate when you askfor advice with the minus numbers. The programsees wins or losses coming and says so; you canalso tell this when the "worth" of a move exceeds55.  Just subtract 55 from the absolute value tosee how many stones the machine expects to win orlose by.HOW IT WORKSKALAH uses the classical Artificial Intelligencemini-max search tree procedure, with Alpha-Betacutoff to eliminate useless searches. KALAHmentally tries all its legal moves, then simulatesall your possible replies to each of them, andthen coniders all its counter-replies, and so onfor L-1 layers, where L is the skill level youchose between 2 and 12 (Now you see why higherskill levels play so slowly!).  At the bottom orLth layer, KALAH simply rates the resulting boardpositions in terms of material; for Pigeons thisis the total number of stones in your bins minusthe total in the computer's; for Kalah the stonecount in its Pit minus your Pit's contents. Thesenumerical values are reported up the search tree,choosing the maximum values at the layers wherethe machine was moving, and minimum value where itwas your turn to move.  Wins and losses have theconstant 55 added to them to make them stand out. KALAH is totally blind beyond the last layer ofmoves tested, and this nearsighted vision givessome strange moves at skill levels below 6.To improve play near the end of a game, KALAHlooks one extra move ahead for each blockedposition (no move) on either side that it finds inthat branch of the search tree.  This almostdoubles the search depth when a player is blockedfor several turns in a row.MAINTAINING THE SOURCE CODEThere are four source files that must be compiledand linked together if you make any changes.KALAH.C is the main() program plus two subroutinesthat get moves for the machine and you. EVAL.C isthe "brains" including the search tree andboard-position evaluators.  In the tradition ofArtificial Intelligence, EVAL is recursive,calling itself for each deeper layer oflook-ahead. SHOW.C displays board positions ineither of two formats depending on whether you'replaying Pigeons or Kalah. MOVE.C includes theprocedure makel() which tests the legality ofmoves and makes them by updating the board array.MOVE.C has been hand-optimizing to the assemblyfile MOVE.A, which should be used in any compiles.To prevent you from compiling MOVE.C and wipingout MOVE.A, MOVE.C has been renamed MOVE.SRC.C.The "direct" variable declarations may have to beremoved if you use a non-Microware C compiler.SUGGESTED IMPROVEMENTSSince you have the source, try adding somenew features to the game.  Some ideas:(1) Problem Mode: you can type in a board positionto see what the program and you can do in thatsituation (easy to add).(2) History: program remembers all moves in a gameand can write them out to a file.  Can later readthat file to resume from any point in that game(as in Problem Mode).(3) Review/Backup: player can look back at thepositions and moves that have occurred so far inthis game, and restart the game from any of them."Let's see what would have happened if only I'dmade that other move..."Or just to re-live last week's great victory.(4) Strike Three: in the original DeadPan game,you were allowed to be blocked a total of threeturns per game; if you missed a fourth turn youlost instantly.  Try adding this rule to Pigeons.(5) Bugs: just because I didn't put any in onpurpose doesn't mean you can't look for them.         Have fun!  Any questions, write:                   Mike Knudsen            